<script>
import launcherSource from 'crm/src/native/launcher.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import reportsPageSource from 'crm/src/reports/index.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import DockBadge from 'crm/src/reports/lib/DockBadge.abide'
import dockBadgeSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/DockBadge.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import dockBadgeTestSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/DockBadge.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import UpdateNotice from 'crm/src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.abide'
import updateNoticeSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import updateNoticeTestSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import WinNotification from 'crm/src/reports/lib/WinNotification.abide'
import winNotificationSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/WinNotification.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import winNotificationTestSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/WinNotification.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/lib/Snippet.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="Ship the CRM as a native app">
        <Code code={`abide native
    abide native --out ~/Applications/CRM.app`} />

        <p><code>abide native [--out]</code> wraps the compiled executable in a macOS
            <code>.app</code> that opens the CRM in a window of its own. Its icon is
            <code>src/public/icon.png</code> · <code>src/public/icon.icns</code>. Its name in the
            Dock, the menu bar and the window is the CRM's <code>APP_NAME</code> where an
            <code>onConfig</code> default sets one, read when <code>abide native</code> runs, and
            the <code>package.json</code> name otherwise. That name always names the executable
            inside, and the identifier macOS keeps the app's settings under. The first launch
            offers Start, which runs the app here, and Connect…, which opens one running at a URL.
            Each launch after it does again what the last one did. With <code>connect: false</code>
            and nothing for the start form to ask, the first launch starts the CRM at once, and
            <code>start: 'immediately'</code> does the same with the defaults whatever the form
            asks.</p>

        <Snippet source={launcherSource} path="src/native/launcher.abide" />

        <p><code>src/native/launcher.abide</code> is no page of the CRM's, and what its
            <code>onLauncher</code> answers sets the window and the forms. The answer is a
            <code>LauncherOptions</code>: the window's size, floor and ceiling, how it is drawn,
            and <code>start</code> or <code>connect</code> set to <code>false</code> to drop a
            form. <code>titlebar: 'hidden'</code> or <code>'inset'</code> draws the page under
            the window's controls. The page then reads <code>--abide-titlebar-height</code> and
            <code>--abide-titlebar-inset</code> to keep clear of them, and is dragged by any
            element marked <code>data-abide-titlebar</code>. The window takes its color from the
            page's own background, and <code>dock: false</code> leaves out the Dock icon and the
            menu bar with it. Markup in the same file
            replaces the launch page, and is rendered with a <code>Launch</code>, whose
            <code>fields</code> hold one <code>ConfigField</code> per input the start form asks
            for.</p>

        <p>The CRM runs on a thread of its own, apart from the window's, so it goes on answering
            while the window is resized or a menu is open. Quitting stops it through
            <code>App.stop</code>, so put shutdown work in <code>onStop</code>: a
            <code>process.on('SIGTERM')</code> in the app never runs. <code>process.exit</code>
            ends the whole app, with its code. There, <code>Bun.isMainThread</code> and
            <code>import.meta.main</code> are <code>false</code>.</p>

        <p>Opened from the Finder, the CRM has no terminal, so what it prints goes to
            <code>~/Library/Logs/crm/crm.log</code>, which opens with a few lines saying how
            to read it. A new file starts at each launch and every 5 MB, and the four before it
            are kept as <code>crm.1.log</code> to <code>crm.4.log</code>, newest first. Give a
            build an <code>APP_DATA_DIR</code> of its own and its log folder is named after that
            folder instead, as what the launcher remembers moves there too.</p>

        <Snippet source={reportsPageSource} path="src/reports/index.abide" from={['const printReport', 'onMenu(', 'watch(']} />

        <p>The reports page adds a Reports menu while it is showing. <code>onMenu</code> is read
            the way markup is, so the menus follow the page's values. Each entry is a
            <code>MenuItem</code>: <code>'separator'</code>, an item with an <code>href</code>, one
            with a <code>MenuRole</code> the platform performs, or a <code>MenuEntry</code> made by
            <code>menuItem</code>. <code>MenuEntry.on</code> files what runs when it is picked, and
            hands back what stops it, which is what <code>watch</code> takes. A menu labelled
            like one already in the bar adds to it, and one labelled
            <code>native()?.name</code> adds a group under About, where Settings… goes. In a
            browser nothing
            picks an item, and the page runs unchanged.</p>

        <p>With <code>menuBarExtra: true</code> the CRM also sits in the menu bar, drawn from
            <code>src/public/icon-menubar.png</code>. A click opens
            <code>src/native/menubar.abide</code> in a panel under it, a page outside every
            layout, <code>panelWidth</code> by <code>panelHeight</code>. Without that file a click
            shows and hides the window. A right click lists Open, the page's menus and Quit, a
            menu labelled with the app's name listing its items directly. Closing the window
            keeps the CRM running up there. Add <code>dock: 'window'</code> and
            the Dock icon and the menu bar come and go with the window, so Quit in the app menu
            closes the window and leaves the CRM in the menu bar, where Quit in the right-click
            menu stops it. A link in the panel with
            <code>target="_blank"</code> opens its page in the window, and an item with
            <code>role: 'window'</code> brings the window forward from any menu.</p>

        <Snippet source={reportsPageSource} path="src/reports/index.abide" from={['{#if !native()}']} />

        <p>A browser has no menu bar, so there the page offers the same print as a button.
            <code>native</code> answers the version of the <code>.app</code> whose window the page
            is in and the name its menu bar shows, and <code>undefined</code> in a browser. The
            window names both in the user
            agent of every request it makes, so the server rendering the page reads the same answer
            the page does, and the button is never drawn in the window and taken away. Any client
            can send that user agent, so it decides what a page shows, never who may do what.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Keep the native app current">
        <Snippet source={launcherSource} path="src/native/launcher.abide" from={['update:']} />

        <p>Each person runs their own copy of the CRM, so a new version has to reach every copy.
            <code>update</code> in what <code>onLauncher</code> answers is the address the copies
            look for one at: as they launch, every hour after, and when Check for Updates… is
            picked from the app menu. A newer version is downloaded beside the app and installed
            when it quits, and Check for Updates… becomes Restart to Install.</p>

        <Code code={`abide native --notes CHANGES.md
    abide native --required --notes CHANGES.md`} />

        <p>Every <code>abide native</code> also writes an <code>updates</code> directory beside
            the <code>.app</code>: the app zipped, and <code>latest.json</code> naming it. Upload
            the directory to the <code>update</code> address, and every copy older than it updates
            itself. A copy never installs a version older than its own, so taking an upload back
            stops new installs of it without undoing the ones that happened. Raise the version in
            <code>package.json</code> to publish the fix.</p>

        <p>The feed is signed. The first bundle makes the key, prints where it put it, and every
            bundle after reuses it. Each copy installs only what that key signed, so a host that
            serves something else installs nothing. Keep a copy of the key: a copy of the app
            trusts it and no other. To bundle on another machine, set
            <code>ABIDE_UPDATE_KEY</code> to what the file holds.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Tell people an update is waiting">
        <Example
            id="update-notice"
            title="`pendingUpdate` — an update waiting for a restart"
            summary="A browser stages no update, so this card stays empty here. Its test plays the window, telling the page of an update and then of a required one."
            files={{
                'src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.abide': updateNoticeSource,
                'src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.test.ts': updateNoticeTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <UpdateNotice />
        </Example>

        <p><code>pendingUpdate</code> holds the update the window has staged as a
            <code>StagedUpdate</code>: its <code>version</code>, its <code>notes</code>, and
            whether <code>--required</code> marked it. <code>StagedUpdate.install</code> restarts
            into it now. A required one opens a dialog that Escape cannot close, and quitting
            installs it too, so no copy goes on without it. The reports page shows the notice; an
            app with updates people must not put off renders it from its root layout, so it stands
            over every page.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Count open deals on the Dock icon">
        <Example
            id="dock-badge"
            title="`navigator.setAppBadge` — a count on the Dock icon"
            summary="A browser tab draws no badge, so here only the line shows. Its test plays the window, which answers the page's badge."
            files={{
                'src/reports/lib/DockBadge.abide': dockBadgeSource,
                'src/reports/lib/DockBadge.test.ts': dockBadgeTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DockBadge />
        </Example>

        <p>The window gives every page the web's own badging API. A count from
            <code>navigator.setAppBadge</code> shows on the Dock icon, a call with no count shows a
            dot, and <code>0</code> or <code>navigator.clearAppBadge</code> takes it off. The
            menu bar extra's panel sets the same badge, and Disconnect clears it. A badge kept
            current whatever page is open belongs in the root layout. In a browser the call is
            the browser's: an installed web app may draw it, and a tab answers nothing, so guard
            it with <code>?.</code> and the page runs unchanged.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Notify people of a win">
        <Example
            id="win-notification"
            title="`Notification` — a deal won, as the platform shows it"
            summary="Here the browser asks for permission and shows it. In the native app the same code asks macOS, and the notification carries the CRM's name and icon."
            files={{
                'src/reports/lib/WinNotification.abide': winNotificationSource,
                'src/reports/lib/WinNotification.test.ts': winNotificationTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <WinNotification />
        </Example>

        <p>The window answers the web's own <code>Notification</code> with the platform's.
            <code>Notification.requestPermission</code> asks once, with the system's prompt, and
            <code>Notification.permission</code> starts each launch at what the person chose. A
            notification shows even while the CRM is in front, one with the same
            <code>tag</code> replaces it, and a click brings the window forward and fires its
            <code>click</code>. Run outside a <code>.app</code>, by <code>bun</code> itself, the
            permission is <code>denied</code>, since macOS keeps notifications per app.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What else the window answers">
        <p>The rest of what a page asks of a browser, the window answers the same way, with no
            code of the CRM's. <code>print</code> opens the print panel as a sheet, and Print
            Report… works as the button does in a browser. A link marked <code>download</code>,
            and an answer sent as an attachment, such as the CRM's CSV exports, save to
            Downloads, numbered where the name is taken, and the page stays put. A file input
            opens the open panel as a sheet, <code>prompt</code> asks in a sheet as
            <code>alert</code> and <code>confirm</code> do, and
            <code>requestFullscreen</code> takes the whole screen. The camera, the microphone and
            the location are there for <code>getUserMedia</code> and
            <code>geolocation</code>, each asked for with the system's prompt the first time.
            <code>navigator.share</code>, the clipboard and <code>navigator.wakeLock</code> are
            WebKit's own and work unchanged.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
